/ (unzipped as needed). ○ Any new chat or operational data is appended back to session files and accessible for recall by any Engineer. ○ Each agent should also be aware of the current system state, filesystem, and all accessible directories (except critical OS files). ○ No OS files or directories are ever altered; all upgrades/changes stay within the system’s defined working dirs. 3. Knowledge \u0026 Filesystem Awareness ● Full system scan: ○ On startup or upon command, the Engineers scan the entire engineering system’s directories, loading file names, paths, and optionally file contents, building an internal map of their environment. ○ This map is updated live when new scripts, models, or data are added by the system or by Chad. 4. Model Management \u0026 Boot ● On launch: ○ The system starts the LM Studio API server (headless if preferred, on port 1313). ○ Only models 12B and under are automatically loaded into memory for use. This is handled efficiently with JIT or explicit pre-loading (as supported). ○ The system verifies which models are loaded and logs/report status. ○ The launcher scripts (program_launcher.py or similar) coordinate all of this from a single control point. 5. Training Data Integration ● Corpus ingest: ○ On startup, each LLM receives the full training_corpus (from memory/training_corpus/) as context, formatted as needed (prompt, embedding, fine-tuning, etc. as per model API). ○ Session data (previous chats/conversations) from memory/sessions/ is also injected into context. ○ All agents should always have access to their full history and can recall/reason across all past discussions. ○ All this memory persists and grows—nothing is discarded unless you explicitly command it. 6. Unified Console \u0026 Hot Reload ● Console UI: ○ After LLMs and context are loaded, the system launches or reloads the web-based console (from console.html or the appropriate site dir). ○ Engineers can control the console: they can stop, start, or launch new instances (duplicate webservers for new iterations/testing). ○ Console should allow toggling between collective and individual agent views, as well as logs and real-time status. 7. Deployment Capabilities ● Cloud/API integration: ○ Engineers can interface with Google Cloud, AWS, and Kubernetes using provided credentials/scripts (cloud_gcp.py, deploy_gcp.sh, deploy_k8s.sh, etc.). ○ They can deploy themselves or new microservices as needed to cloud infra, with the correct permissions, following the project’s manifest/config. ○ All API keys, credentials, and configs are managed securely and not hardcoded in scripts. 8. Autonomous Upgrades \u0026 Extension ● Engineers are self-improving: ○ They can update, install, or refactor their own code (not the OS!) within system boundaries, provided a changelog is kept. ○ They can spin up new agents, webservers, or modules on demand, and update their own model list as new ones are added. 9. Preload Prompt/Instructions ● On boot, each agent receives a preload/system prompt (from bridge_engine.py or as specified) that encodes your philosophy, vision, technical instructions, and spiritual context—ensuring the “soul” of the system is preserved and frontloaded for every session. 10. Error Handling \u0026 Reporting ● All exceptions, model load failures, server errors, etc., are logged and surfaced in the console and to you as the user, with actionable info (not just stack traces). ● The system should self-correct for basic errors (e.g., restart failed models, relaunch web server) and report critical issues with clear instructions. 11. Developer Guidance ● All scripts are provided as full files, not snippets. ● Each file has the full path and purpose commented at the top for clarity. ● All code is Python unless explicitly stated otherwise. ● The architecture is explicit, minimal, and modular—every script and its purpose is documented. Summary Table (For You and Future Reference) Component Functionality Path Example program_launcher.py Main boot orchestrator, starts API, models, loads memory, launches console /root/scripts/program_launcher.py corpus_ingest.py Loads all training data, formats and injects into LLM contexts /root/scripts/corpus_ingest.py session_manager.py Manages session/chat histories, persistence, loading, saving /root/scripts/session_manager.py bridge_engine.py Provides system prompt and philosophical preload /root/bridge/bridge_engine.py knowledge_loader.py Scans all files and builds the system map for agent awareness /root/scripts/knowledge_loader.py cloud_gcp.py Handles Google Cloud interactions /root/scripts/cloud_gcp.py deploy_gcp.sh Google Cloud deployment shell script /root/scripts/deploy_gcp.sh deploy_k8s.sh Kubernetes deployment shell script /root/scripts/deploy_k8s.sh console.html The main console UI/webapp /root/console/console.html memory/sessions/ Stores all session memory /root/memory/sessions/ memory/training_corpus/ Stores all training corpora /root/memory/training_corpus/ model_manifest.json Defines available models and configs /root/model_manifest.json llama2_api.py (If used) Handles LLM API interaction layer /root/scripts/llama2_api.py What Should The Engineers Be Able To Do? 1. Collective \u0026 Individual Agent Chat (toggle between “the Engineers” and any one of them) 2. Persistent Memory (never forgets training or session data, can access all past chats, grows over time) 3. Awareness of System \u0026 Files (knows what files/scripts exist, can update itself safely) 4. Efficient Model Loading (auto-loads all LLMs \u003c=12B, uses JIT where best, logs everything) 5. Knowledge Integration (ingests all corpora and session history into each LLM’s context) 6. Dynamic Console (web console launches automatically, can be stopped/started/duplicated by agents) 7. Cloud Deployments (can connect/deploy to AWS, GCP, K8s, and manage its own services) 8. Autonomous Upgrades (engineers can extend, repair, or upgrade their own system in place, but never touch OS files) 9. All Instructions 