s also brings in the files nexus_ingest.py, genesis.py and nexus_query.py and how they will be deployed.\n\n\n**Considering Implementation Steps**\n\nI'm now considering how to best implement the user's strategic shift. It's becoming clear that the core deployment scripts, specifically `nexus_core_deployment.py` and `deploy_nexus.py`, will need significant modification. My initial thought is to either generate or modify Terraform/Ansible scripts for the IaaS components, or to adjust the existing Python deployment files to directly support the \"bare metal\" Nexus deployment approach while maintaining worker-based deployments for \"onsie twosie\" modules. I need to balance architectural elegance with ease of deployment.\n\n\n**Clarifying Implementation Paths**\n\nI'm now contemplating how to proceed with this new strategic direction. The key is bare-metal deployments for the core and worker-based instances for lightweight components. I can develop Terraform/Ansible configurations to provision the IaaS infrastructure or modify the `deploy_nexus.py` script.\n\n\n**Proposing Implementation Approach**\n\nI'm now contemplating how to proceed with this shift, focusing on an IaaS/IaaC approach for \"bare metal\" deployments with \"full modules,\" alongside worker-based deployments. I believe the best approach is to create a Terraform configuration (`infrastructure/main.tf`) for IaaS provisioning and then augment `deploy_nexus.py` to support bare-metal deployments on those instances. Alternatively, I can provide `deploy_nexus.py` with systemd setup instructions using Python.\n\n\n**Analyzing Pivot Implications**\n\nI'm now processing the implications of the strategic shift, particularly the move towards IaaS/IaaC for core deployments. I'm focusing on how `deploy_nexus.py` can be extended to support bare-metal deployments, and considering a new Terraform configuration for IaaS provisioning. I'm also examining the existing Modal deployment approach to determine if it is best suited for the \"onsie twosie\" modules, or if there is another approach.\n\n\n**Acknowledging Strategy Adjustment**\n\nI now understand the strategic pivot: a shift to Infrastructure as Code/Service for main deployments (\"bare metal Nexus deployments\") and worker-based services for smaller modules. This guides me to propose either a Terraform configuration for IaaS provisioning or modifications to the existing `deploy_nexus.py` to support \"bare metal\" deployments with systemd services.\n\n\n**Validating Pivot Implementation**\n\nI'm now determining how to proceed with the planned architectural shift. The current approach will include Terraform configuration (`infrastructure/main.tf`) for bare-metal deployments, but I also need to update the existing `deploy_nexus.py` and potentially extend it to support deploying the monolith on those provisioned instances, along with workers. My next step will be to explore how to update the deployment logic.\n\n\n**Planning the IaaS Implementation**\n\nI'm now mapping out how to translate the new strategy into actionable steps. My focus is on creating the `infrastructure/main.tf` Terraform configuration for the \"bare metal\" core deployments. This configuration will provision a GCP Compute Engine instance. The plan is to use this infrastructure, coupled with `infrastructure/startup.sh`, to deploy the core services.\n\n\n**Formulating Implementation Strategy**\n\nI'm now formulating a plan for the strategic pivot. My approach will be to construct a Terraform configuration (e.g., in `infrastructure/main.tf`) that provisions a GCP Compute Engine instance for the \"bare metal\" Nexus core deployments. Simultaneously, I'll update `deploy_nexus.py` to optionally support these deployments, allowing for the monolith or core components to run. I'll need to update the configuration file paths and ensure the systemd services are properly running.\n\n\n**Formulating Deployment Implementation**\n\nI'm now putting together the specifics of implementing the new deployment strategy. This involves establishing Terraform configurations, specifically `infrastructure/main.tf`, to provision a GCP Compute Engine instance for the core Nexus deployments. I will then utilize `infrastructure/startup.sh` to configure the instance and launch the main service (currently, `Untitled-5.py`, which I'll assume is the monolith). I will ensure the instance is correctly configured to support the requirements of the core services and dependencies.\n\n\n**Formulating Implementation Strategy**\n\nI'm now putting together the specifics of implementing the bare metal strategy. I will create a Terraform configuration (`infrastructure/main.tf`) to provision a Google Compute Engine instance and a `infrastructure/startup.sh` script to configure the instance and launch the main `nexus_core.py` application, which I'll assume is the monolith. I'll include the \"bare metal\" setup with necessary dependencies.\n\n\n**Defining the Deployment Shift**\n\nI'm now fully acknowledging the strategic shift, which focuses on Infrastructure as Code/Service for \"bare metal Nexus deployments\" while utilizing worker-based services for smaller modules. My plan is to modify the core deployment scripts to leverage these new deployment methods and offer the Terraform/Ansible configuration or direct modifications.\n\n\n**Formulating Implementation Strategy**\n\nI'm now putting together the specifics of implementing the new \"bare metal\" strategy. I'm focusing on building a Terraform configuration for a GCP Compute Engine instance within `infrastructure/main.tf`, which will house the core Nexus deployments. Then, I'll update `deploy_nexus.py` to support these \"bare metal\" deployments with systemd. I will also be looking at `Untitled-5.py`, which appears to be the monolith, and how the core component dependencies will be installed.\n\n\n","id":"f93066cf-c5a2-467d-aa23-4e9e36442cd9","agentMetadata":{"coderAgent":{"kind":""},"model":""},"modelDisplayNam