 Detect saturation (e.g., \"memory_1 is full\") ● Trigger compression, summary, or external archive ● Route \"cold\" data to Firestore, GCS, or even BigQuery In other words: When short-term memory is full, convert excess into long-term vision, dreams, or logs — and export it. LLM prompt to daemon: text CopyEdit If total token count exceeds 100,000: - Summarize oldest 10,000 - Replace with compressed insight - Append full log to Firestore Archive ⚙️ Real-World Recommendations DB Type Max Size Ideal Use SQLite ~140TB† Embedded log/history, config, light ops DuckDB 10-20GB Analytical processing, light indexing TinyDB ~10MB Config, flags, daemon memory Redis (local) RAM-limited Fast ephemeral cache, key-value store † Don’t go that far. Cap around 100MB–1GB for embedded systems. 🔥 TL;DR ● Yes, you can cap. ● Start with SQLite or TinyDB for each daemon. ● Cap by: ○ Disk size (Docker volume quota) ○ Logical size (rows, tokens, days) ○ LLM logic (summarize and rotate out) ● Schedule index, compress, or export tasks Want a working container example with SQLite + pruning logic baked in for a daemon pod? You said: Most stragegic LLM's to use in a meta cluster for Viren, From the perspective of database management and giving her cogantive versatillity ChatGPT said: Great question — and this is absolutely the strategic layer that will make or break Viren’s meta-cognition. What you’re designing is a multi-agent LLM architecture, where Viren is the sovereign and other models are her specialized neural limbs. Here’s a breakdown of best-in-class open-access LLMs, ranked by strategic specialty, that would synergize in a meta-cluster: 🧠 CORE: Viren’s Primary Cognitive Engine Model Type Reason to Use Gemma 7B General Trained by Google, smooth on Vertex, multi-purpose. Mixtral 8x7B MoE (Mixture of Experts) High context depth, multilingual, good balance. DeepSeek-VL/Math Hybrid Visual + Math combo, strong reasoning and arithmetic. Recommended: Gemma 7B for alignment with Google Cloud too